{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792150706000-architect-6da-stops-glm.glm-5-3.findings.md; sed -n '1,210p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc; rg -n 'fuel-drive|check-returned|boundary-round|sampled-returned|every-returned|layout-corpus' test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; cat test/yin/vm/ucf/ledger_fixtures.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 11:51:29 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 18:51:29 +07\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: e33f308d-2ca8-4019-917d-4c85854673b4\n# Task: architect rulings on the three stops of the 6d-a implementation — glm glm-5.3 verdicts (resumed thread)\nRole: Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: glm-5.3 | Assigned: 2026-10-16 18:40 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: acting Architect for codex-authored work while Fable is out (handoff §3); final message promoted verbatim below (two CLI warning lines dropped); stdout at collab/1792150706000-architect-6da-stops-glm.glm-5-3.stdout.log\n\nAll three stops verified in the implementation worktree. Rulings:\n\n# Architect rulings on the three 6d-a stops (glm, resumed thread; read-only, nothing changed)\n\nVerification basis: the failing test and its corpus (`layout_test.cljc:737-746`, `safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1840-1845`, `corpus.cljc:110`), the driver (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:2147-2176`) and row 20's builder (`:500-508`), the discharge refusal shape (matches `missing-obligations` and the existing ghost pin at `:2314-2322`), the ledger golden's rendered shape (`test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` — fixed literal authority data, carrier-derived BLAKE3 addresses embedded in baselines), and `target/6da/root-remints.txt`.\n\n## Ruling 1 — Acceptance 9 set equations vs dead ambient operands: **(a) restrict the equation's domain; do NOT implement the dead-prefix lift in 6d-a**\n\nThe four failures are genuine and by construction, not a bug in the fix: `:resume-operand` is `(f (resume :p x) y)` (`corpus.cljc:110`) — at pc 1 (`L={}`) and pc 2 (the `:resume`, `uses={3}`) the enclosing application's operator (register 1) is in the static layout but dead, because `:resume` is abortive (the call site's stack and frames are dropped, `stack.cljc:765-772`); `:tail-operand` is `(f (tail (g x)) y)`, added at phase 5 with a docstring that already documents \"dead layout syntax reads registers other than the call's own\" (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1841-1843`). The static layout is *correct* — the value really is on the stack; it is the equation's identification of region content with the live set that fails at pcs whose remaining enclosing reads a tail transfer or abort abandons. One correction to the question's framing: **6e does not close this** — the dead-prefix rule governs the region *after* the transfer (the dead ambient becomes the prefix below the callee's layout); at these pre-transfer operand pcs the static equality is *permanently* false, and 6e's ordered dynamic checks are what carry them.\n\n**Acceptance-9 replacement text (execute verbatim):**\n\n> **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity and jump/terminator layout availability; at every reachable tail-call PC check `set(uses(p)) = L(p)`; at every other reachable PC check the inclusion `set(uses(p)) ⊆ L(p) ⊆ set(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p))`, and additionally the equality `set(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p)) = L(p)` everywhere **except the pinned dead-ambient operand PCs — exactly `{(:resume-operand 1), (:resume-operand 2), (:tail-operand 1), (:tail-operand 2)}`** — where an operand-position `:resume` (abortive) or tail call (the enclosing application's call is unreachable) makes the enclosing application's pushed operands dead per L while they remain in the static layout and on the stack. At the pinned PCs assert the exclusion's cause: the layout entries outside `L(p)` are exactly those dead ambient operands. The exclusion set is enumerated, never predicate-based: a future corpus entry with a dead ambient layout fails the equality loudly rather than being skipped. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`. No dead-prefix lift, `slice-window` widening, or running-backend lift is implemented in 6d-a; those remain 6e item 6's scope.\n\n**Plan §3.5 sentence to append (records the deferral):**\n\n> **Dead ambient operands (static domain).** The non-tail set equation holds where the enclosing application's pending reads are still reachable. At the operand PCs of an application whose evaluation an operand-position tail call or `:resume` abandons (the corpus's `:resume-operand` and `:tail-operand` fixtures), ambient layout entries are dead per L while remaining in the region: the strongest static truth there is the inclusion `uses(p) ⊆ L(p) ⊆ layout(p) ∪ uses(p)`; the equality is permanently excluded at those pcs. 6e's dead-prefix rule governs the region after the transfer, and 6e's ordered dynamic checks — not the static set equality — carry those pcs.\n\n## Ruling 2 — Row 20 setup snapshot: **measure from the first discharge-satisfied boundary; exclude-and-pin the leading refusal; `discharge!` is NOT changed**\n\nVerified: `fuel-drive` observes S₀ (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:2150`); row 20's setup batch (`:500-505`) defines `s` and `c` by runtime `s/def!` store-puts, so at S₀ the root store lacks `s` and conservative discharge refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied :incomplete :obligations #{s}` — the same derive-don't-persist conservatism the owner ratified unchanged under default 5 (the pre-branch `ghost` pin at `:2314-2322` is the family's existing precedent). Admitting future store definitions is a definite-assignment analysis over the CFG — new authority in the portability contract, out of 6d-a scope; do not touch `discharge!`.\n\n**Harness formulation (execute verbatim):** in `check-returned-boundaries`/`fuel-drive`, a boundary whose lift refuses `[:yin.k/unsatisfied :incomplete]` is excludable **only while no boundary of the same case has lifted successfully** (a contiguous leading run). For each excluded boundary: assert zero serves; record its pc and its `:yin.k/missing :obligations`; assert the obligations are names the case's own program defines later (row 20: exactly `#{s}`). After the first successful lift, any refusal is a hard failure. Pinned per-case counts report measured and excluded-leading boundaries separately; row 20's excluded set is exactly `[S₀, obligations #{s}]`.\n\n**Acceptance-text sentence (append to item 7):**\n\n> Row 20's setup batch (`s/def!` store definitions executed at runtime) lifts only from the first boundary at which its definitions are answered in the store; the leading boundary is excluded as a **pinned conservative-discharge false refusal of the owner-ratified Q6 family (default 5: keep edge-reachability, derive don't persist)** — recorded per case with its obligations and zero serves, never skipped silently: the driver asserts the excluded boundaries form a contiguous leading run and that every boundary after the first successful lift lifts.\n\n## Ruling 3 — Protected ledger golden: **regenerate — it is the grammar-consequent re-mint of the same class as the rows fixtures, under four conditions**\n\nVerified: `ledger-v2.txt` is a deterministic rendered journal (header, `projection <digest>`, one hex-CBOR transaction per line) of a scripted custody scenario whose authority data is fixed literals (occurrences `…-01/-02/-04`, holders `holder-a/b`, leases `lease-1..4`, sequential op-ids); the only carrier-derived content is the BLAKE3 segment addresses embedded in the `:yin.k/bid`/`:yin.k/root`/`:yin.k/baseline` records — exactly what the grammar change moves (task slots 17/18 change every task row's canonical bytes, hence every root id and pack address, hence every baseline and the projection digest). Conditions, all mandatory:\n\n1. Regenerate **through the existing render route only** — never hand-edit the golden.\n2. The old→new change must be **address/digest-only**: every non-address field (occurrence ids, holders, leases, epochs, op-ids, kinds, policies, positions, intents, values) byte-identical. Retain a line-level or field-classified old/new diff inventory beside the report in `target/6da` (the recorded digests prove the change happened, not that it is innocent) — Fable's Tuesday diff-confirm reads it.\n3. Account for the **six frame-byte equality failures** in that same inventory: each must compare bytes that embed a moved address. A standalone frame pack's own bytes must NOT change (`:act`/`:tail` grammar is untouched — the unmoved worked-frame pin corroborates this). If any of the six compares a frame whose own bytes changed, stop again: that would not be grammar-consequent.\n4. Old/new projection digests and golden hashes stay recorded in the report (done: `419e3aa6… → 52f8127f…`, `5de18ed0… → 59a6944d…`).\n\n## Embedded question — the nineteen inline task-root pins: **confirmed within the authorized re-mint; none to revert**\n\n`root-remints.txt` shows 19→19 pairs, all `:segment/blake3-…` task-root ids — exactly the class v2.1 default 1 and acceptance item 14 authorize (\"remint affected roots after module-pins commit 2\"; \"remint fixtures from the integrated grammar\"). Corroborating: the count is stable, no equality assertion was removed, and the standalone worked-frame pin did **not** move — consistent with task rows moving while `:act`/`:tail` frame packs stay byte-stable. The `:call-id` fixture correction (`:parked-0` rather than nil) is an assertion-target fix, not a pin remint; keep it flagged in the report for Fable's diff-confirm.\n\nThese rulings unblock acceptance items 7, 9 and 14; the engineer resumes against the remaining work listed in the report's table, and the next gate remains the full diff review after the resumed run finishes — these rulings cover only the three stops and the pins question, as instructed.\n(ns yin.vm.semantic.layout-test\n  \"Phase 6c acceptance 1-9 (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md\n   §8.3): the register pairing and live sets, the register destination from\n   A, the stack correspondence, operand layout and K slices, E\n   reconstruction, closures both ways, and the unobservable fill of a\n   lowered tail. The static items run over C, the B0 corpus and the\n   phase-3 corpus (derive-test's wider C requires the Python ANTLR lane);\n   the dynamic items run the harness's worlds on the de Bruijn lanes.\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [clojure.walk :as walk]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.corpus :as corpus]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.layout :as layout]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as linearize]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test :as h]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-support :as s]))\n\n\n(defn retained-ffi-refusal\n  [lane]\n  (doseq [source h/lanes]\n    (testing (str (name source) \" -> \" (name lane))\n      (h/scoped\n        (fn []\n          (let [world (h/on-lane source #(h/retained-ffi identity))\n                entry (first (:wait-set (:machine world)))\n                {:keys [toy serve!]} (h/served (:surfaces world))\n                wire (h/lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)\n                task (rows/decode wire rows/default-budget)\n                pending (second (:slots (first (nth (:slots task) 13))))\n                expected {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :retained-ffi}\n                {:keys [attach!] n :count} (h/recording-attacher toy)\n                receiver ((:receiver world))]\n            (is (= :put (:reason entry)))\n            (is (true? (:request-sent entry)))\n            (is (= :ffi-request (first (:slots pending))))\n            (is (= expected (layout/lower-defect task lane)))\n            (is (= expected (h/error #(h/lane-lower lane (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver)) wire attach!))))\n            (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\"))))))\n  (testing \"an FFI response wait without a retained request still lowers and continues\"\n    (h/scoped\n      (fn []\n        (let [world (h/on-lane :semantic #(h/sent-ffi identity))\n              {:keys [serve!]} (h/served #{:reader})\n              wire (h/lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)]\n          (is (nil? (:request-sent (first (:wait-set (:machine world))))))\n          (is (nil? (layout/lower-defect (rows/decode wire rows/default-budget) lane))))))\n    (let [got (h/crossed :semantic lane #(h/sent-ffi identity))]\n      (is (pos? (:attaches got)))\n      (is (:halted got))\n      (is (= \"echo!\" (:value got))))))\n\n\n(deftest stack-refuses-retained-ffi-before-attach\n  (retained-ffi-refusal :stack))\n\n\n(deftest register-refuses-retained-ffi-before-attach\n  (retained-ffi-refusal :register))\n\n\n(def C\n  \"[label ast]: the harness's layout corpus.\"\n  h/layout-corpus)\n\n\n(defn projected\n  [ast]\n  (:vector (linearize/project ast)))\n\n\n(defn reachable-pcs\n  \"Every pc reachable from its body's start.\"\n  [v]\n  (into #{} (mapcat #(analysis/reachable v (:start %))) (code/body-ranges v)))\n\n\n(defn boundary?\n  [t]\n  (contains? rcode/boundary-opcodes (nth t 0)))\n\n\n(defn parking?\n  \"A non-tail parking instruction (rule A1).\"\n  [t]\n  (or (contains? #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation} (nth t 0))\n      (and (= :call (nth t 0)) (not (code/tail-call? t)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 1-2: the register kernel's static layout\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest register-pairing\n  (let [checked (atom [0 0])]\n    (doseq [[label ast] C]\n      (testing (str label)\n        (let [v (projected ast)\n              sg (layout/segment v)\n              bodies (:bodies sg)]\n          ;; phys is total on every body's named virtuals and single-valued\n          (doseq [[pc x s] (:pairs sg)]\n            (is (= s (layout/phys sg pc x)) (str \"pc \" pc \" virtual \" x)))\n          (doseq [b (range (count bodies))]\n            (let [named (into #{} (keep (fn [[pc x]] (when (= b (code/body-index bodies pc)) x))) (:pairs sg))]\n              (is (= named (set (keys (nth (:phys sg) b)))))))\n          ;; virt is single-valued on live-out's physical image\n          (doseq [p (sort (reachable-pcs v))\n                  :when (boundary? (nth v p))]\n            (let [lo (layout/live-out sg p)]\n              (is (= (count lo) (count (set (map #(layout/phys sg p %) lo))))\n                  (str \"distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots at pc \" p))\n              (doseq [x lo]\n                (is (= x (layout/virt sg p (layout/phys sg p x))))))\n            (swap! checked update 1 inc))\n          (swap! checked update 0 inc))))\n    (is (= (count C) (first @checked)))\n    (is (< 50 (second @checked)) \"reachable boundaries checked\")))\n\n\n(deftest register-live-is-live-out\n  (let [tails (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[label ast] C]\n      (testing (str label)\n        (let [v (projected ast)\n              sg (layout/segment v)]\n          (doseq [p (sort (reachable-pcs v))\n                  :let [t (nth v p)]\n                  :when (boundary? t)]\n            (let [live (nth (nth (:instructions (:register sg)) p) (rcode/live-slot-index (nth t 0)))]\n              (is (= (layout/register-live sg p) live) (str \"pc \" p))\n              (when (code/tail-call? t)\n                (swap! tails inc)\n                (is (= [] live (layout/register-live sg p)) \"a tail boundary's live is []\")))))))\n    (is (pos? @tails))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 3: rd from A, never the physical destination\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def worked-ast (second (first (filter #(= :worked-example (first %)) corpus/programs))))\n\n\n(defn in-world\n  \"(f machine) of the world build makes on lane, its construction and its\n   layout lookups in one h/scoped: no other fixture's vector is in its\n   space.\"\n  [lane build f]\n  (h/scoped #(f (:machine (h/on-lane lane build)))))\n\n\n(defn lifted-waits\n  \"The canonical wait entries of world machine m: the evaluator's own, or a\n   de Bruijn kernel's through the layout. m's kernel-space is its scope's.\"\n  [m]\n  (if (= :semantic (h/lane-of m))\n    (:wait-set m)\n    (:wait-set (layout/canonical-task m (h/kernel-space m) (h/on-lane :semantic h/machine)))))\n\n\n(deftest register-rd-from-a\n  (testing \"the worked example parked at its inner call\"\n    (h/scoped\n      (fn []\n        (let [m0 (h/on-lane :register h/machine)\n              [sref m1] (engine/attach-resource m0 (s/ring 4))\n              [cref m2] (engine/handle-cursor m1 {:stream sref} :k1)\n              next! (engine/resolve-var {} {} vm/primitives h/stream-modules 'stream/next!)\n              m (vm/run (h/load-program (assoc m2 :free-env {'f (get vm/primitives 'conj) 'g next! 'x cref 'y 2})\n                                        worked-ast))\n              entry (first (:wait-set m))\n              [p] (lifted-waits m)\n              v (projected worked-ast)]\n          (is (vm/blocked? m))\n          (is (= [:call 2 3 [4] false] (nth v 3)) \"the inner call\")\n          (is (= 4 (:pc p)))\n          (is (= {:deliver :rd :rd 2} (:deliver p)) \"the canonical rd is A's 2\")\n          (is (= 3 (:dest entry)) \"the kernel's dest is the physical 3\")\n          (is (= #{1} (set (keys (:window p)))) \"saved(4, 2)\")))))\n  (testing \"the K frames of :depth-3 take rd from A\"\n    (let [[_ wrap] (nth h/segments 2)\n          build #(h/reader wrap h/read-op)\n          reference (first (:wait-set (:machine (h/on-lane :semantic build))))\n          [m [p]] (in-world :register build (juxt identity lifted-waits))\n          entry (first (:wait-set m))]\n      (is (= :depth-3 (first (nth h/segments 2))))\n      (is (= 6 (count (:k p))) \"three bindings, three nest applications\")\n      (is (= (mapv :rd (:k reference)) (mapv :rd (:k p))) \"the evaluator's return destinations\")\n      (is (some (fn [[f kf]] (not= (:rd f) (:dest kf))) (map vector (:k p) (:continuation entry)))\n          \"some physical dest differs from its virtual rd\"))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 4-5: the stack correspondence and operand layout\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn family\n  [op]\n  (case op (:load-bound :load-free) :var op))\n1840:(def layout-corpus\n1873:        layout-corpus))\n2071:(defn boundary-round-trip\n2103:            {:keys [recv peer wire]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n2123:          {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip m)\n2132:        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n2147:(defn fuel-drive\n2159:(defn check-returned-boundaries\n2165:                          (boundary-round-trip m))\n2167:                result (with-redefs [vm/run #(fuel-drive % observe)] (reference (build)))]\n2172:(deftest sampled-returned-boundaries\n2174:        counts (check-returned-boundaries cases)]\n2179:(deftest ^:slow every-returned-boundary\n2180:  (slow/guard \"every-returned-boundary\"\n2182:                (let [counts (check-returned-boundaries (matrix-cases))]\n2193:          {:keys [wire recv peer]} (boundary-round-trip m)\n2212:        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip nonportable)\n2234:        {:keys [recv wire]} (boundary-round-trip source)]\n2246:        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip m)\n2256:        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip (engine/check-wait-set m))\n2276:        {:keys [wire recv peer]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n2292:        {:keys [recv]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n2303:        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip (assoc completed :halted? true :blocked? false :value 42))\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixtures\n  \"The cross-host ledger fixture of M-next C slice C12: one arbitration\n   ledger holding every fact kind of yin.vm.ucf.ledger, built by a\n   portable script (`build`) and pinned as its journal frames.\n\n   The pins live in test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt: a first\n   line `ledger-v2`, a second `projection <digest>`, the BLAKE3 hex of\n   the canonical bytes of the projection the frames fold to, then one\n   journal frame per line as lowercase hex, header first.  Regenerate\n   the file from a one-off JVM script with the string `render` returns,\n   saved to `path`; the test proves on every host that the file equals\n   `render`, so every host's rebuild has the pinned bytes.\n\n   Version 2 (phase 7a): the offered and reported checkpoints are\n   version-3 row packs (yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-fixtures), each stored\n   and recorded under its pack address, its root id inside the baseline.\n\n   Nothing here writes a file.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n            [dao.lease :as lease]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.journal :as journal]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority :as authority]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission :as admission]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion :as completion]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant :as grant]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.input :as input]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-fixtures :as fx]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.custody :as custody]))\n\n\n(def path\n  \"The fixture file, relative to the repository root.\"\n  \"test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt\")\n\n\n(def arb \"arb-c12\")\n\n(def duration {:s 30})\n\n\n;; The root, its continuation successor and a stranger occurrence.\n(def r \"0c000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\")\n\n(def s1 \"0c000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002\")\n\n(def x \"0c000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004\")\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; World: the requests a holder and the composition make\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn header-frame\n  \"The journal header frame naming identity `i`.\"\n  ([] (header-frame arb))\n  ([i]\n   (cbor/encode {:dao.stream.journal/header {:version 1 :identity i}})))\n\n\n(defn origin\n  [o l]\n  {:yin.k/occurrence o :dao.lease/lease l :yin.k/emitter \"holder-a\"})\n\n\n(defn park\n  \"A blocked root of occurrence `o` with counter `n` and origin `org`,\n   nil for a first park.\"\n  ([o n] (park o n nil))\n  ([o n org]\n   (fx/edit-custody (fx/base \"first-park\")\n                    #(cond-> (assoc %\n                                    :yin.k/occurrence o\n                                    :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n                                    :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity arb\n                                                        :dao.stream/descriptor\n                                                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/journal}})\n                       (some? org) (assoc :yin.k/origin org)))))\n\n\n(defn halted\n  \"A halted result of occurrence `o` under lease `l`.\"\n  [o l]\n  (fx/edit-custody (fx/base \"halted-root\") #(assoc % :yin.k/origin (origin o l))))\n\n\n(defn world\n  \"A fresh world: the authority's frames (an atom), its content store and\n   a diagnostic stream.\"\n  []\n  {:frames (atom [(header-frame)])\n   :store (mem/create-content-mem)\n   :diag (:dao.stream/handle\n           (ringbuffer/create! {:dao.stream/type ringbuffer/transport-type\n                                ringbuffer/capacity-key 64}))})\n\n\n(defn offer!\n  [{:keys [a store]} b]\n  (let [bs (cbor/encode b)]\n    (grant/offer! a store (fx/segment-address bs) bs \"carrier\")))\n\n\n(defn grant!\n  [{:keys [a]} o l h]\n  (stream/append! (grant/writer a)\n                  (lease/grant l (custody/subject o) h duration\n                               {:dao.lease/proposal (str \"p-\" l)})))\n\n\n(defn lapse!\n  [{:keys [a]} l cause]\n  (stream/append! (grant/writer a) (lease/lapsed l cause)))\n\n\n(defn refuse!\n  \"The hook's refusal of `proposer`'s proposal pid, through the writer.\"\n  [{:keys [a]} proposer pid]\n  (stream/append! (grant/writer a)\n                  (assoc (lease/refusal pid) :yin.k/proposer proposer)))\n\n\n(defn report!\n  \"holder-a reports body `b` as o's successor under l.\"\n  [{:keys [a]} o l b]\n  (let [bs (cbor/encode b)]\n    (completion/report! a \"holder-a\"\n                        (completion/resumed o l (fx/segment-address bs))\n                        bs)))\n\n\n(defn envelope\n  [l e o n v]\n  {:yin.k/envelope :yin.k/fenced-v1\n   :yin.k/incarnation l\n   :yin.k/epoch e\n   :yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence o :yin.k/seq n}\n   :yin.k/value v})\n\n\n(defn admit!\n  [{:keys [a store i diag]} l e o n v]\n  (admission/admit! a store i \"holder-a\" (envelope l e o n v) diag))\n\n\n(defn record!\n  [{:keys [a]} o l e k observed]\n  (input/record-input! a \"holder-a\"\n                       (input/request o l e k\n                                      {:yin.k/kind :yin.k/read :yin.k/name \"s\"}\n                                      observed)))\n\n\n(defn read-ok\n  [v pos]\n  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   :dao.stream/value v\n   :dao.stream/cursor {:position pos}})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The script\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn answer\n  \"One request's answer as a status keyword.\"\n  [rep]\n  (or (:yin.k/status rep) (:yin.k/admission rep) (:dao.stream/outcome rep)))\n\n\n(def script-answers\n  \"The answer each step of `build` gives, in order.\"\n  [:committed                           ; enroll target i\n   :committed                           ; offer r\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; grant lease-1 on r\n   :recorded                            ; input 0 of r\n   :committed                           ; admit (r 0)\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; refuse holder-b's p-b\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; reclaim lease-1, epoch 1\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; grant lease-2 on r\n   :committed                           ; report s1\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; release lease-2: r completes\n   :committed                           ; offer s1\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; grant lease-3 on s1\n   :committed                           ; report a halted result\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; release lease-3: s1 terminates\n   :committed                           ; offer x\n   :dao.stream/ok                       ; grant lease-4 on x\n   :committed                           ; admit (x 0) :v\n   :intent-conflict                     ; admit (x 0) :w: x quarantined\n   :committed])                         ; close target i\n\n\n(defn build\n  \"Run the script on a fresh memory-backed authority.  Answers the world\n   with `:answers`, one per step, and the authority, left open.\"\n  []\n  (let [w0 (world)\n        a (::authority/authority\n            (authority/open! (journal/memory-backend (:frames w0) nil)))\n        enrolled (authority/enroll! a)\n        w (assoc w0 :a a :i (:yin.k/target enrolled))\n        steps [#(offer! w (park r 0))\n               #(grant! w r \"lease-1\" \"holder-a\")\n               #(record! w r \"lease-1\" 0 0 (read-ok 7 1))\n               #(admit! w \"lease-1\" 0 r 0 :v)\n               #(refuse! w \"holder-b\" \"p-b\")\n               #(lapse! w \"lease-1\" :policy)\n               #(grant! w r \"lease-2\" \"holder-a\")\n               #(report! w r \"lease-2\" (park s1 3 (origin r \"lease-2\")))\n               #(lapse! w \"lease-2\" :release)\n               #(offer! w (park s1 3 (origin r \"lease-2\")))\n               #(grant! w s1 \"lease-3\" \"holder-a\")\n               #(report! w s1 \"lease-3\" (halted s1 \"lease-3\"))\n               #(lapse! w \"lease-3\" :release)\n               #(offer! w (park x 0))\n               #(grant! w x \"lease-4\" \"holder-a\")\n               #(admit! w \"lease-4\" 0 x 0 :v)\n               #(admit! w \"lease-4\" 0 x 0 :w)\n               #(authority/close-target! a (:i w))]]\n    (assoc w :answers (into [(answer enrolled)] (map #(answer (%))) steps))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Rendering and reading\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn digest\n  \"The BLAKE3 hex of the canonical bytes of projection `p`.\"\n  [p]\n  (jing/digest-bytes :blake3 (cbor/encode p)))\n\n\n(defn hex\n  [bs]\n  (cfx/bytes->hex bs))\n\n\n(defn render\n  \"The fixture file's text, computed from `build` on this host.\"\n  []\n  (let [{:keys [a frames]} (build)]\n    (str \"ledger-v2\\n\"\n         \"projection \" (digest (authority/projection a)) \"\\n\"\n         (str/join (map #(str (hex %) \"\\n\") @frames)))))\n\n\n(defn read-file\n  \"The fixture file as `{:digest d :frames [bytes ...]}`.\"\n  []\n  (let [[magic pin & frames] (remove str/blank?\n                                     (str/split-lines (cfx/read-path path)))]\n    (when-not (= \"ledger-v2\" magic)\n      (throw (ex-info \"not a ledger-v2 fixture\" {:path path})))\n    {:digest (subs pin (count \"projection \"))\n     :frames (mapv cfx/hex->bytes frames)}))\n\n\n(defn read-text\n  \"The fixture file's text.\"\n  []\n  (cfx/read-path path))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '715,770p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc; sed -n '2010,2200p' test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; cat deps.edn | head -100; rg -n '§3.5|3.5 |Layout gates|Row 20|Layout' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '715,770p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc; sed -n '2010,2200p' test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; cat deps.edn | head -100; rg -n '§3.5|3.5 |Layout gates|Row 20|Layout' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"                (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\"))\n              (let [got (h/crossed :semantic lane build)]\n                (is (pos? (:attaches got)))\n                (is (= (count (h/stream-identities (:wire got))) (:attaches got)))\n                (is (= (h/normalize expected) (:value got)) \"admitted, and used through the collection\"))))\n          (testing (str \", lifted from \" (name lane))\n            (if procedure?\n              (h/scoped\n                (fn []\n                  (let [m (:machine (h/on-lane lane build))\n                        n (atom 0)\n                        {:keys [serve!]} (h/served #{:reader})\n                        d (h/error #(h/lane-lift m (fn [x] (swap! n inc) (serve! x))))]\n                    (is (= lane (h/lane-of m)))\n                    (is (vm/blocked? m))\n                    (is (= [:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n                    (is (= 'box (:yin.k/key d)))\n                    (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\"))))\n              (is (= (h/normalize expected) (:value (h/crossed lane :semantic build)))\n                  \"admitted, and used through the collection\"))))))))\n\n\n(deftest static-layouts-at-every-pc\n  (doseq [[label ast] C]\n    (let [v (projected ast) sg (layout/segment v) a (:analysis sg)]\n      (doseq [p (sort (reachable-pcs v))]\n        (testing (str label \" pc \" p)\n          (is (vector? (layout/stack-layout sg p)))\n          (is (= (analysis/live a p)\n                 (if (code/tail-call? (nth v p))\n                   (set (code/uses (nth v p)))\n                   (into (set (layout/stack-layout sg p)) (code/uses (nth v p)))))))))))\n  (doseq [build [#(linking identity false true)\n                 #(linking identity false false)\n                 #(installing identity false nil)]\n          malformed [nil :true 1 \"true\"]]\n    (let [{:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader :writer})\n          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine (build)) serve! (lift-opts))\n          bad (rows/emit (update-at (root-task wire) [13 0 1]\n                                    #(update % :slots (fn [xs] (assoc xs (dec (count xs)) malformed)))))\n          attached (atom 0)\n          result (error #(rows/lower-task (machine) bad (fn [_] (swap! attached inc))))]\n      (is (= :yin.k/undecodable (:yin.k/status result)))\n      (is (= :pending-try (:yin.k/kind result)))\n      (is (zero? @attached) \"malformed flags are refused before resource attachment\"))))\n\n\n(deftest park-beside-a-live-reader-wait\n  (let [[reader src] (parked-reader)\n        parked (run-beside (assoc reader :env (:free-env reader)) {:type :vm/park})\n        pid (:id (vm/value parked))\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n        wire (rows/lift-task parked serve! (lift-opts))\n        {:keys [attach!]} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach!}) wire attach!)\n        resumed (run-beside recv {:type :vm/resume :parked-id pid :val (s/lit \"unparked\")})]\n    (is (= 1 (count (:wait-set recv))) \"the reader survives beside the park\")\n    (is (contains? (:parked recv) pid) \"the explicit park survives independently\")\n    (is (= \"unparked\" (vm/value resumed)))\n    (is (not (contains? (:parked resumed) pid)))\n    (is (= 1 (count (:wait-set resumed))) \"resuming the park does not consume the reader\")\n    (stream/append! src \"B\")\n    (let [woken (sweep resumed [peer])\n          done (vm/run woken)]\n      (is (empty? (:wait-set done)))\n      (is (vm/halted? done))\n      (is (= \"B\" (vm/value done))))))\n\n\n(deftest noise-before-the-correlated-ffi-response\n  (let [call-in (s/ring 8) call-out (s/ring 8)\n        m (caller identity call-in call-out)\n        id (ffi/response-call-id (first (:wait-set m)))\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n        {:keys [attach!]} (recording-attacher toy)\n        own-in (s/ring 8) own-out (s/ring 8)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach! :call-in own-in\n                                        :call-out own-out :call-out-cursor (vm/mint-oldest own-out :test)})\n                              wire attach!)]\n    (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response :unrelated \"noise\"))\n    (let [waiting (drive-peers recv [peer])]\n      (is (vm/blocked? waiting) \"an unrelated response cannot wake this call\")\n      (is (= 1 (count (:wait-set waiting))))\n      (is (= id (ffi/response-call-id (first (:wait-set waiting)))))\n      (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response id \"echo!\"))\n      (let [done (drive-peers waiting [peer])]\n        (is (empty? (:wait-set done)))\n        (is (vm/halted? done))\n        (is (= \"echo!\" (vm/value done)))\n        (is (empty? (tu/drain own-in)) \"the carried request is not sent again\")))))\n\n\n(defn boundary-round-trip\n  \"Snapshot without execution, retaining the peer for later continuation.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [{:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served #{:reader :writer})\n        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n        {:keys [attach! origin] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach!}) wire attach! install-lower-opts)\n        again (served #{:reader :writer})\n        relift (rows/lift-task recv (:serve! again) (lift-opts))\n        corr (into {} (map (fn [[id _]] [id id])) @(:table peer))\n        corr2 (into {} (map (fn [[id e]] [id (get @origin (:handle e))])) @(:table (:peer again)))]\n    (is (= (rows/canonical-under wire corr) (rows/canonical-under relift corr2)))\n    (is (nil? (rows/validate-frames (root-task wire))))\n    {:wire wire :recv recv :peer peer :attaches @n}))\n\n\n(deftest completed-link-and-install-outcomes\n  (doseq [install? [false true] try? [false true] success? [false true]]\n    (testing (pr-str [install? try? success?])\n      (let [world (if install? (installing (try-wrapper try?) false nil)\n                      (linking (try-wrapper try?) false false))\n            m (:machine world)\n            completed (if install?\n                        (if success?\n                          (do ((first (:steps world))) (engine/check-wait-set m))\n                          (engine/abandon-installs m :test-abandoned))\n                        (let [entry (first (:wait-set m))\n                              resp (get (:resources m) module/link-response-resource)]\n                          (if success?\n                            ((first (:steps world)))\n                            (stream/append! resp {:yin.link/id (:link-id entry) :status :refused :reason :absent}))\n                          (engine/check-wait-set m)))\n            {:keys [recv peer wire]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n            source-outcome (try {:value (normalize (vm/value (drive-local completed)))}\n                                (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                                  {:error (ex-data e)}))\n            received-outcome (try {:value (normalize (vm/value (drive-peers recv [peer])))}\n                                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                                    {:error (ex-data e)}))]\n        (is (empty? (:installs completed)))\n        (is (seq (:ready-queue completed)))\n        (is (= source-outcome received-outcome))\n        (is (= 1 (count (slot (root-task wire) 18))))))))\n\n\n(deftest task-products-and-ready-validation\n  (doseq [both? [false true] active? [false true]]\n    (let [[m call-out id] (two-callers)\n          _ (if both? (answer-both! call-out id)\n                (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response id \"first\")))\n          swept (engine/check-wait-set m)\n          m (if active? (sr/run-fuel swept 1) swept)\n          {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip m)\n          t (root-task wire)]\n      (is (= (if active? :running :blocked) (slot t 2)))\n      (is (= (if both? 0 1) (count (slot t 13))))\n      (is (= (if active? 1 0) (count (slot t 17))))\n      (is (= (- (if both? 2 1) (if active? 1 0)) (count (slot t 18))))))\n  (let [[m call-out id] (two-callers)\n        _ (answer-both! call-out id)\n        completed (engine/check-wait-set m)\n        {:keys [wire]} (boundary-round-trip completed)\n        t (root-task wire)]\n    (is (= 1 (quot (count (:slots (slot t 9))) 2)) \"one shared cursor identity\")\n    (is (= [\"second\" \"first\"] (mapv #(apply2/response-ok (:value %)) (:ready-queue completed))))\n    (doseq [[mutate kind]\n            [[#(assoc-in % [:slots 18 0 :slots 3] \"invalid\") :ready-outcome]\n             [#(assoc-in % [:slots 18 0 :slots 5] [:lit :unexpected]) :ready-outcome]\n             [#(assoc-in % [:slots 18 0 :slots 1 :slots 0] :unknown) :row-shape]\n             [#(assoc-in % [:slots 18 0 :slots 4] (rows/node :cursor-cells [:missing (second (:slots (slot t 9)))])) :slot-kind]]]\n      (let [n (atom 0)\n            d (error #(rows/lower-task (machine) (rows/emit-numbered (mutate t) {}) (fn [_] (swap! n inc))))]\n        (is (= kind (:yin.k/kind d)) (pr-str d))\n        (is (zero? @n))))))\n\n\n(defn fuel-drive\n  \"Drive to the next guest stop, observing each returned budget boundary.\"\n  [m observe]\n  (observe m)\n  (loop [m m n 0]\n    (when (> n 20000) (throw (ex-info \"Boundary driver failed to stop\" {:steps n})))\n    (let [next (sr/run-fuel m 1)]\n      (observe next)\n      (if (or (:control next) (seq (:ready-queue next)))\n        (recur next (inc n)) next))))\n\n\n(defn check-returned-boundaries\n  [cases]\n  (mapv (fn [[row segment _ build]]\n          (let [points (atom 0)\n                observe (fn [m]\n                          (swap! points inc)\n                          (boundary-round-trip m))\n                expected (reference (build))\n                result (with-redefs [vm/run #(fuel-drive % observe)] (reference (build)))]\n            (is (= expected result) (str row \" \" segment))\n            [row segment @points])) cases))\n\n\n(deftest sampled-returned-boundaries\n  (let [cases (filterv #(contains? #{1 19 20 22} (first %)) (matrix-cases))\n        counts (check-returned-boundaries cases)]\n    (is (= 13 (count counts)))\n    (is (every? pos? (map #(nth % 2) counts)))))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow every-returned-boundary\n  (slow/guard \"every-returned-boundary\"\n              (fn []\n                (let [counts (check-returned-boundaries (matrix-cases))]\n                  (is (= 89 (count counts)))\n                  (println \"6d-a returned-boundary counts JVM:\" (pr-str counts))))))\n\n\n(deftest completed-put-history-and-shape-only-ids\n  (doseq [op-id [nil (s/op-id 0)]]\n    (let [world (writer identity)\n          _ ((first (:steps world)))\n          m (engine/check-wait-set (:machine world))\n          m (cond-> m op-id (assoc-in [:ready-queue 0 :op-id] op-id))\n          {:keys [wire recv peer]} (boundary-round-trip m)\n          t (root-task wire)]\n      (is (= {} (:yin.k/ops (rows/custody-baseline t))))\n      (is (= \"v\" (vm/value (drive-peers recv [peer]))))\n      (doseq [bad-id [{} (assoc (s/op-id 0) :yin.k/seq -1)]]\n        (let [bad (assoc-in t [:slots 18 0 :slots 1 :slots 3] bad-id)\n              {:keys [attach!] n :count} (s/attacher)\n              d (error #(rows/lower-task (machine) (rows/emit bad) attach!))]\n{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \"1.8.741\"}\n        org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \"1.14.1\"}\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \"1.0.333\"}\n        org.replikativ/boring {:mvn/version \"0.1.30\"}\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \"1.0.7364\"}\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \"42.7.3\"}\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \"2.5.0\"}\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \"1.18.3\"}\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\n        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\n        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \"0.4.6\"}\n        io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 {:mvn/version \"1.3\"}\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\"\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\n :cljd/opts {}\n ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\n ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\n :paths [\"src/clj\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/cljs\" \"src/cljd\"\n         \"build/antlr/python3/classes\"]\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                                        {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                           :main-opts [\"-m\" \"btree-bench\"]}\n           :test {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\n                               {:git/tag \"v0.5.1\" :git/sha \"dfb30dd\"}}\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cognitect.test-runner\"]\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \"1.0.0\"}\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"nrepl.cmdline\" \"--middleware\" \"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\"]}\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                               com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}}\n           :dev {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                              cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}\n                              reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                              com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}\n                              ;; benchmark baseline + psset-fixtures\n                              ;; generator only; production uses\n                              ;; dao.data.btree\n                              persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                              {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                 :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]}\n           ;; The one ClojureDart pin. Compose it with the build aliases:\n           ;; clj -M:clojuredart:cljd ..., clj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build ...\n           :clojuredart {:extra-deps {sonwh98/clojuredart\n                                      {:git/url \"https://github.com/sonwh98/ClojureDart.git\"\n                                       :sha \"7d00ee2aa7fa9d990581e78ebc4a66b93cb70a31\"}\n                                      }}\n           :cljd {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n                  :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :server {:extra-deps {http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}}\n                    :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]\n                    :main-opts [\"-m\" \"world.server\"]}\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.main\"]}\n           :cljs-yin-repl\n           {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}\n            :main-opts [\"-m\" \"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\" \"run\" \"yin.repl.main/-main\"]}\n           :fmt {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :exec-fn tasks/fix-all}\n           ;; Build-time generation of the grammars-v4 Python3 parser.\n           ;; The tool version must equal the antlr4-runtime above.\n           :antlr-gen {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                       :extra-deps {org.antlr/antlr4 {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}}\n                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"yang-antlr-gen\"]}\n           :kondo {:extra-deps {clj-kondo/clj-kondo {:mvn/version \"2026.01.19\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"clj-kondo.main\"]}\n           :dbg {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                 :main-opts [\"-m\" \"debug-cljd\"]\n                 :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :cljd-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.runner\"]}\n           :cljd-yin-repl-build\n           {:main-opts  [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n            :cljd/opts  {:kind       :dart\n                         :main       yin.repl.main\n                         :output-dir \"build/yin-repl\"\n                         :output-to  \"build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart\"}}\n           :dep-graph {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                       :extra-deps {org.clojure/tools.namespace {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}}\n                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"dep-graph\"]}}}\n\n\n;; clj -X:fmt\n\n;; clj -M:nREPL\n\n307:### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow PCs; the register side\n537:9. **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity, jump/terminator layout availability and separate non-tail/tail set equations. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`.\n568:Retain v2’s scope and acceptance, replacing references to its oracle and cardinality equation with §§3.5–3.6 above. Ready handling uses the completed-outcome grammar, not pending-wait reconstruction. The backend fragment and existing obstruction list remain unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '215,360p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc; sed -n '300,340p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '510,552p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '1,140p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '215,360p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc; sed -n '300,340p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '510,552p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '1,140p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"    (testing (str label)\n      (let [v (projected ast)\n            sg (layout/segment v)\n            h (:stack sg)]\n        (is (= (count v) (count (:a->h sg))) \"one non-push H instruction per A pc\")\n        (is (= (count h) (+ (count v) (count (filter #(= :push (first %)) h)))))\n        (doseq [k (range (count v))]\n          (is (= (nth (nth v k) 0) (family (nth (nth h (nth (:a->h sg) k)) 0)))\n              (str \"the k-th non-push instruction, k = \" k))\n          (is (= k (get (:h->a sg) (nth (:a->h sg) k)))))))))\n\n\n(deftest stack-layout-is-the-saved-set\n  (let [checked (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[label ast] C]\n      (testing (str label)\n        (let [v (projected ast)\n              sg (layout/segment v)\n              a (:analysis sg)]\n          (doseq [p (sort (reachable-pcs v))\n                  :let [t (nth v p)]\n                  :when (parking? t)]\n            (is (= (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd t)) (set (layout/stack-layout sg p))) (str \"pc \" p))\n            (is (apply distinct? (cons ::bottom (layout/stack-layout sg p))))\n            (swap! checked inc)))))\n    (is (< 20 @checked)))\n  (testing \"the worked cases\"\n    (let [first-call (fn [v] (first (keep-indexed (fn [pc t] (when (= :call (first t)) pc)) v)))]\n      (doseq [ast [worked-ast\n                   (corpus/app (corpus/v 'f) (corpus/if-node (corpus/v 'c) (corpus/app (corpus/v 'g)) (corpus/lit 2))\n                               (corpus/v 'y))]]\n        (let [v (projected ast)\n              sg (layout/segment v)]\n          (is (= [1] (layout/stack-layout sg (first-call v))) \"[f], f in register 1\"))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 6: K slices partition the operand stack\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn stack-frames\n  \"[[A-pc slice] …] for return frames then the current activation of\n   stack entry e of kernel k, reading the kernel's own offset table.\"\n  [k e]\n  (let [sp (h/kernel-space k)\n        a-pc (fn [abs]\n               (let [[ident rel] (dvm/image-pc k abs)\n                     sg (some #(when (= ident (:stack-id %)) %) (vals (:segments sp)))]\n                 [sg (inc (get (:h->a sg) (dec rel)))]))\n        parts (layout/slices (vec (:stack e)) (mapv :stack-base (:continuation e)))]\n    (mapv (fn [abs slice] (conj (a-pc abs) slice))\n          (conj (mapv :return-pc (:continuation e)) (:pc e))\n          parts)))\n\n\n(defn non-tail?\n  \"6c-pre marks a tail call's park :return-result.\"\n  [e]\n  (not= :return-result (:resume-mode e)))\n\n\n(deftest stack-slices-partition\n  (let [checked (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row label _ build] (h/lane-cases)\n            :when (contains? #{:bare :depth-3 :unwrapped} label)]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n        (in-world :stack build\n                  (fn [k]\n                    (doseq [e (concat (:wait-set k) (vals (:parked k)))\n                            :when (non-tail? e)]\n                      (let [frames (stack-frames k e)]\n                        (is (= (vec (:stack e)) (into [] (mapcat #(nth % 2)) frames)) \"the slices partition the stack\")\n                        (doseq [[sg pc slice] frames]\n                          (is (= (count (layout/stack-layout sg (dec pc))) (count slice))))\n                        (swap! checked inc)))))))\n    (is (< 20 @checked)))\n  (testing \"the multi-frame fixture\"\n    (h/scoped\n      (fn []\n        (let [expr (h/cat-of (s/lit [1])\n                             (s/app (s/lam ['x] (h/cat-of (s/lit [2])\n                                                          (s/app (s/lam ['y] (h/cat-of (s/lit [3]) h/read-op))\n                                                                 (s/lit 2))))\n                                    (s/lit 1)))\n              world (h/on-lane :stack #(h/reader identity expr))\n              k (:machine world)\n              e (first (:wait-set k))\n              st (:stack e)]\n          (is (= [0 0 0 2 4] (mapv :stack-base (:continuation e))) \"the bases, pinned from the kernel\")\n          (is (= 6 (count st)))\n          (is (= [[] [] [] 2 2 2] (mapv #(if (empty? %) [] (count %)) (layout/slices (vec st) [0 0 0 2 4]))))\n          (is (= [[1] [2] [3]] (mapv #(nth st %) [1 3 5])) \"each slice holds its activation's cat and literal\")\n          (is (every? values/closure? (map #(nth st %) [0 2 4])))\n          (is (= (vec st) (into [] cat (layout/slices (vec st) [0 0 0 2 4]))))\n          (let [sp (h/kernel-space k)\n                p (layout/lift-state (layout/lift-context k sp (:owner k)) e)\n                fresh (layout/attach-space (h/on-lane :stack h/machine) sp)\n                lowered (layout/lower-state (layout/lower-context fresh sp) p)]\n            (is (= 5 (count (:k p))))\n            (is (= [0 0 0 2 4] (mapv :stack-base (:continuation lowered))) \"the lowering emits the same bases\")\n            (is (= 6 (count (:stack lowered))))\n            (is (= (h/normalize (:stack e)) (h/normalize (:stack lowered))))\n            (stream/append! (s/stream-of k) \"B\")\n            (is (= [1 [2 [3 \"B\"]]] (h/normalize (vm/value (h/drive-local (assoc fresh :wait-set [lowered] :blocked? true\n                                                                                :halted? false :value :yin/blocked\n                                                                                :resources (:resources k))))))\n                \"and it continues\")))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 7: E reconstruction\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def env-programs\n  \"The B2 duplicate-parameter, nested-closure and nested-lambdas shapes,\n   each blocked on a read inside its innermost body.\"\n  (let [read-into (fn [& xs] (reduce (fn [acc x] (h/cat-of acc x)) (s/lit []) (concat xs [h/read-op])))]\n    [[:duplicate-param (s/app (s/lam ['x 'x] (read-into (s/v 'x))) (s/lit 1) (s/lit 2)) [2 \"B\"]]\n     [:nested-closure (s/app (s/app (s/lam ['a 'a] (s/lam ['b] (read-into (s/v 'a) (s/v 'b)))) (s/lit 1)) (s/lit 2))\n      [nil 2 \"B\"]]\n     [:nested-lambdas (s/app (s/app (s/lam ['a] (s/lam ['b] (read-into (s/v 'a) (s/v 'b)))) (s/lit 1)) (s/lit 2))\n      [1 2 \"B\"]]]))\n\n\n(defn envs\n  \"Every E of a machine's canonical waits and parked records, outermost\n   K frame first, values normalised.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [waits (lifted-waits m)\n        parked (if (= :semantic (h/lane-of m))\n                 (vals (:parked m))\n                 (vals (:parked (layout/canonical-task m (h/kernel-space m) (h/on-lane :semantic h/machine)))))]\n    (h/normalize (mapv (fn [p] (conj (mapv :env (:k p)) (:env p))) (concat waits (sort-by :id parked))))))\n\n\n(deftest env-reconstruction\n  (let [checked (atom 0)\n        cases (into (mapv (fn [[row label _ build]] [(str \"row \" row \" \" (name label)) build]) (h/lane-cases))\n                    (map (fn [[label ast]] [(name label) #(h/reader identity ast)]))\n                    env-programs)]\n    (doseq [[label build] cases]\n      (testing label\n        (let [expected (envs (:machine (h/on-lane :semantic build)))]\n          (doseq [lane [:stack :register]]\n            (is (= expected (in-world lane build envs)) (str (name lane) \"'s E is the evaluator's\"))\n            (swap! checked inc)))))\n\n`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n\nRunning install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-1 bounds bridge execution; glm’s inactive-predicate omission is closed explicitly.\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow PCs; the register side\n\n**Replace v2’s displayed ordered equation and its explanatory paragraph. Retain jump/terminator derivation, stutter normalization, dead-prefix treatment and register allocation requirements.**\n\nLet:\n\n```text\nregister-sequence(p) = layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)\nexpected-values(p)   = [W[r] for r in register-sequence(p)]\n```\n\nAfter the separately specified dead-prefix treatment, compare the kernel region to `expected-values(p)` in emitter order. Its length equals the length of `register-sequence(p)`. Repeated occurrences of a virtual register occupy repeated positions and must decode to agreeing values.\n\nSeparately check:\n\n```text\nset(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p)) = L(p)   at reachable non-tail PCs\nset(uses(p))                 = L(p)   at reachable tail-call PCs\n```\n\nThere is no requirement that the length of the ordered sequence equal `|L(p)|`. Static checks validate layout availability and register sets; dynamic checks validate actual region length, order and repeated-register agreement.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-6 removes the false cardinality equation without weakening ordered reconstruction.\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Replace the coverage and refusal-oracle paragraphs; retain v2’s lift → lower → immediate relift → law 6 → execution order and isolated worlds.**\n\n**Coverage.**\n\nThe stepping driver uses returned `run-fuel 1` boundaries. Its coverage classes are body entry, taken and untaken branch, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture, resume, park, FFI park, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, and running install child.\n\nBlocked-plus-ready is tested through an explicit **post-sweep/pre-resume fixture**, constructed with `check-wait-set`. It is not a mandatory returned boundary of the stepping driver. The shared-cursor fixture uses this same seam.\n\n- Add run, ready and completion rows; task slots 17/18; complete lift/lower/restore paths for every existing queue producer.\n- Implement update-body encoding and role-aware census traversal.\n- Factor operation-ID shape validation and invoke it from slot-18 completed validation.\n- Extend backend pre-attachment refusal traversal to ready values.\n- Add jump/terminator layouts and corrected static gates.\n- Propagate fuel through scheduler/install execution; use bounded bridge dispatch.\n- Add the closed-gate guard and cover all semantic execution entries.\n- Update the affected design text and fixtures with these behaviors.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n\n1. **Frame rules.** Run states at worked-example boundaries validate, including body start. Pin PC kind/bounds, window scope, missing/extra keys, forbidden deliver/pending and wrong-context diagnostics. C3 failures on act/return frames are `:pc-body`. Delivery frames use `saved(pc, rd)`; running frames use `L(pc)`.\n\n2. **Row grammar and completed validation.** Round-trip run frames; reject extra slots and incorrect reference kinds. Ready states admit act/tail only. Status is nil or keyword. Pin invalid completion tags, site mismatch, nonboolean link/install `try?`, inconsistent refusal slots, missing update-cell membership, duplicate update keys and malformed completed operation IDs. These lower refusals occur before attachment.\n\n3. **Task-product kinds.** Retain running-only, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, blocked-plus-ready and halted-plus-ready cases. Add halted-plus-outstanding-wait with empty queue: pin the existing blocked normalization and dropped result separately. Retain invalid run counts, illegal result/parked-id and empty-blocked cases.\n\n4. **Completed link/install outcomes.** Exercise success, try failure and ordinary raised failure for link/install producers. Capture completed install outcomes after removal of the install entry. Include active control beside a remaining completion. Immediate relift is equal; continuation delivers or raises once, without reconstructing retired pending resources.\n\n5. **Shared cursor.** Two waiters share one cursor. Snapshot after the sweep before any resume. Preserve one canonical cell identity, the slot-9 current body, both values and update rows in order. Assert immediate relift equality and source/receiver continuation equality. Add an encoding case where one C pairs with different bodies across census/update rows.\n\n6. **Completed write and terminal outcomes.** Fork and exclusive completed-write histories round-trip without enrollment or reappend. A malformed present ID refuses at the named slot-18 check. Nil IDs remain admissible where the ungated source produced none. Add a terminal-status ready case whose receiver raises identically, plus the FFI sent-request transition that creates a response wait without a second request append.\n\n7. **Every returned boundary, slow tier.** Run the existing corpus through `run-fuel 1`; lift, lower into isolated worlds, immediately relift, validate frames and continue to the reference outcome. Pin measured counts and the driver coverage classes of §3.6. Constructed post-sweep cases are reported separately.\n\n8. **Quick sample and products.** Retain v2’s structural sample and every-boundary small rows. Run the scheduler products and shared-cursor seam through the harness. Retain cursor-ref, reified-continuation, nil-live-register, `ghost` and hold-refusal cases.\n\n9. **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity, jump/terminator layout availability and separate non-tail/tail set equations. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`.\n\n10. **Budgeted children.** Preempt long and nested initializers; lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked. Count actual instruction transitions and assert at most `n` per visited task and `n × t` overall. No child receives a second budget inside one invocation.\n\n11. **Bounded bridge execution.** A program repeatedly making bridge-served FFI calls returns from every `run-fuel` invocation. Each invocation executes at most one raw action and one bridge step. Repeated invocations reach the reference result. Diagnostic-only bridge input also returns after one step. Pin control-less ingress-ready dispatch to the budgeted round. For a pure active computation lasting at least `n` instructions, compare with `n` single-instruction steps; an earlier park/halt ends the comparison there.\n\n12. **Fencing.** Lower a running snapshot with `{:fenced true}` and exercise direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel`. Each refuses the closed gate before guest, scheduler or bridge work. Assert unchanged control/window/environment/K, unchanged ready recovery data and unchanged relift bytes. Cover `:ended` as well as `:exporting`.\n\n13. **Ready-value portability.** The only unsupported primitive is nested in the ready outcome; register-target lower refuses before attachment. Retain the corresponding portable positive case. Test ready delivery state, outcome and restored descriptor roots independently.\n\n14. **Landing checks.** Kondo clean; the quick tier passes on JVM, Node and Dart with every touched namespace present in logs; the slow tier runs once at landing on all three hosts. Remint fixtures from the integrated grammar. Review stops on satisfied gates, not a fixed round count.\n\n**Change:** All three P1s and all reconciled P2/P3 acceptance amendments are now explicit 6d-a gates.\n\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
