{:rule [:completion :op-id-shape], :counts {:test 8, :pass 223, :fail 12, :error 0}, :killed? true}\nMUTANT {:rule [:install :phase-child-kind], :counts {:test 8, :pass 229, :fail 6, :error 0}, :killed? true}\nMUTANT {:rule [:task :kind-table], :counts {:test 8, :pass 214, :fail 21, :error 0}, :killed? true}\nMUTANT {:rule [:semantic :closed-gate], :counts {:test 2, :pass 28, :fail 24, :error 0}, :killed? true}\nExecution error (AssertionError) at user/eval30645 (r2-mutation-campaign.clj:53).\nAssert failed: A weakening mutant survived\n(every? :killed? results)\n\nFull report at:\n/var/folders/n0/xl3_1xkn0yd5trpq843l81840000gn/T/clojure-8487470882903766701.edn\n**Backend refusal traversal.**\n\n`lower-defect` inspects the slot-17 run through `state-prim`. For each slot-18 row it explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls `state-prim` on that row. It also calls `value-prim` on the outcome `:V` and optional refusal value. Any value-bearing completion fields that will be restored as backend payload receive the same traversal.\n\nThis replaces v2’s double-dereference expression. With the corrected grammar there is no intermediate wait row to dereference.\n\nThe independent test oracle traverses the same semantic roots independently of implementation helpers. Existing image-collision, free-environment, install and retained-FFI backend limits remain. Unsupported ready values must refuse before attachment.\n\n**Export classification.**\n\n`export/kind-of` follows the complete §3.1 precedence, including ready-only blocked tasks. Recovery metadata and validity checks include the ordered ready list. Export/abort/reopen preserve the list rather than executing it.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-4 adopts glm N1’s independent update-body encoding and muse N2’s shared-cursor interpretation; P2-7 and glm N2 fix ready-value traversal and cref resolution.\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Replace outcome 2, outcome 4 and the tests paragraph; retain outcomes 1 and 3 and explicit hand-off policy.**\n\n**2. Tenure dies after a completed slice.**\n\n`run-cycle` becomes a sequence of stage-local transitions. A stage receives the latest holder state and returns either a completed successor state or an explicit failure outcome containing the latest completed state.\n\n```text\nstage-result =\n  {:status :ok,     :state completed-state}\n  or\n  {:status :failed, :state latest-completed-state, :failure failure}\n```\n\nEach stage:\n\n1. Checks tenure against its input state.\n2. Executes its operation, retaining the existing tenure checks at external seams.\n3. Associates a successfully returned machine and stage metadata into a successor state.\n4. Passes that successor to the next stage.\n\nThe stage-local catch closes over that stage’s input state. A later tenure failure therefore returns the state containing the previous stage’s completed machine. It does not fall back to `step-active`’s original argument.\n\nThe failure consumer calls `end-run` with the state carried in the failure outcome. `end-run` retains its machine, gates it `:ended`, and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary` diagnostics. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their existing propagation rules; they are not converted into ordinary program failures.\n\nIf an operation throws before returning a machine, retain the last completed stage, not an invented partial result. External effects already performed are not rolled back. No retained boundary receives new execution or publication authority.\n\n**4. Reopen and fencing.**\n\nA fenced running restore keeps control, window, environment and K, with gate `:exporting`. The kernel entry guard is already present from **6d-a**. It refuses `:exporting` and `:ended` before executing an instruction or entering a scheduler/bridge path.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` are covered. The guard remains outside the instruction loop. `rehydrate-fenced` must reproduce the published pack byte for byte, including ordered completed outcomes and cursor updates.\n\n**Tests, 6d-b.**\n\nRetain the five clock-controlled cuts from v2: before the slice, immediately after it, during replay/emit/read, between slices, and during running-root export. At each cut assert the **exact retained machine**, not merely its PC or the presence of diagnostics. A failure inside a stage retains its input machine; a failure after a completed stage retains its output machine.\n\nKeep the source fenced or ended, forbid effects after tenure loss, and verify either an authorized successor or recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain competing-candidate, uncertain-abort, expired-grant and recovery-byte-equality cases. Repeat the 6d-a execution-entry fencing checks through the holder’s reopened object.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-8 supplies an explicit exception-state channel; P1-2 moves fencing into 6d-a while retaining lifecycle tests in 6d-b.\n2527:## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\n2931:## 11. Owner questions\n2434:7. **Every returned boundary, slow tier.** Run the existing corpus through `run-fuel 1`; lift, lower into isolated worlds, immediately relift, validate frames and continue to the reference outcome. Pin measured counts and the driver coverage classes of §3.6. Constructed post-sweep cases are reported separately. Row 20's setup batch (`s/def!` store definitions executed at runtime) lifts only from the first boundary at which its definitions are answered in the store; the leading boundary is excluded as a **pinned conservative-discharge false refusal of the owner-ratified Q6 family (default 5: keep edge-reachability, derive don't persist)** — recorded per case with its obligations and zero serves, never skipped silently: the driver asserts the excluded boundaries form a contiguous leading run and that every boundary after the first successful lift lifts.\n2990:- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n3078:**Q6 ruling.** Keep reachable by control-flow edges, never by value. At the pc before a constant-dead branch, `ghost` refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with obligations `#{ghost}`. This is the conservative discharge rule, not constant propagation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_135","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\np='target/6da/r2-mutation-campaign.clj';s=open(p).read().replace('yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test/install-child-phase-kind-matrix])','yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test/install-child-phase-kind-matrix\\\\n                 yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test/ready-site-mismatch-and-halted-product])');open(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport subprocess\np='docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md';s=open(p).read();old=subprocess.check_output(['git','show','HEAD:'+p],text=True);a=s.index('## 10. Findings');b=s.index('## 11. Owner questions');oa=old.index('## 10. Findings');ob=old.index('## 11. Owner questions');history=old[oa:ob];history=history.replace('## 10. Findings and proposed amendments','## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\\\\n\\\\n**Historical amendment record.** The phase-specific findings below retain their original text. The any-PC rules in §§1–7 and §8.6 supersede earlier exhaustive state, context and eligibility lists.');s=s[:a]+history+s[b:];\ns=s.replace('### 7.4 Fuel','''### 7.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n\nAn exclusive running, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only. A completed \"'`:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write. Completed outcomes never acquire publication or execution authority from their history.\n\nStage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\nProgress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it. Stage-local holder transitions, tenure cuts and durable export are implemented and tested by 6d-b. The 6d-a carrier admits running custody subjects and retains fenced recovery data.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` refuse `:exporting` and `:ended` before guest, scheduler or bridge work. The guard is outside the instruction loop. A fenced running restore preserves control, window, environment, K and ordered completed outcomes for byte-identical relift; nothing runs during lower.\n\n### 7.4 Fuel'\"''',1)\nneedle='Every kind restores slot 18 in order.';s=s.replace(needle,'''Restore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\n'''+needle,1)\n# Keep the prescribed wording beside the existing item numbering.\ns=s.replace('### 5.7 The stepping driver','''Check ordered region length and contents against \"'`layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)`, with repeated-register agreement and the specified dead-prefix treatment. Check the non-tail and tail set equations separately. Ordered multiplicity is not the cardinality of `L(p)`. Retain the parked-window check.\n\n### 5.7 The stepping driver'\"''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md';s=open(p).read().replace('kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted','kind             ; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted').replace('(:blocked, :parked)','(:running, :blocked, :parked)').replace('   custody]         ; plain, see below','   custody          ; plain, see below\\\\n   run              ; slot 17: one :run for :running, none otherwise\\\\n   ready]           ; slot 18: ordered :ready rows, none for :halted');open(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
