{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_139","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '245,270p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; rg -n '\"'^6d-a|''^FAIL|''^ERROR'\"' target/6da/r2-focused-b.log | head -4; rg -n 'yin.k/retained-boundary|7.3 Custody' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; tail -7 target/6da/r2-selected.log\"","aggregated_output":"**Static layouts** (`layout.cljc` `stack-layouts`, 6d-a): for a parse node `:if` at `b` with jump pc `j`, result register `rd(if)` (the `rd` the arms define; the emitter writes both arms to it, `derive.cljc:81-85` for H, the A linearizer for the register), define `layout(j) = layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]`; for a terminator pc (`:return r`, `:halt r`) define `layout = []`; every other pc as today. Then at every pc inside a body:\n\n```\nordered:  region(p) = layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses-in-order(p)]        ; emitter order (derive.cljc:92-97): :call operator then operands, :ffi-call operands, :stream-put target then value\nset:      (set layout(p)) ∪ (set uses(p)) = L(p)   for every reachable non-tail pc\n          (set uses(p)) = L(p)                     for every reachable tail-call pc\n```\n\nThe ordered form is the check (duplicates `(f x x)` are two positions holding one value; the lift asserts they agree). `jump` and `branch-false` pcs: branch pc keeps the ancestors' layout (`uses` is the test, which H pops); jump pc as above. **`:push` pcs of H**: canonicalised, not refused: `stack-layout` at a `:push` is undefined because it is no A pc; the kernel state at a `:push` equals the state at its successor except pc (`stack.cljc:722-723`); `h->a` maps `hpc` to `h->a(hpc+1)` when `hpc` is a `:push` (never two pushes in a row, `correspondence` `:97-108`); the lift relabels, running nothing; laws 4/5 compare at the aligned A boundary. The design calls this a stutter of the stack backend, not a property of A.\n\n**Dead prefix (tail residuals)**: a tail `:call` consumes `f` and its args and keeps the rest of the region (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`) without pushing a frame (`:737-738`). Hence inside a body entered by a tail call, and transitively, the current activation's region and any later frame's slice may carry a **dead prefix** below the layout: values the ambient layout syntax pushed, never read again (every H read is top-relative: `:call` by `f-pos` from the top, `:branch-false`/`:return`/`:halt` peek; `return-transition` truncates to `stack-base`, which includes the prefix, and the prefix stays dead). Rule: `lift-stack` takes the **top** `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the rest (`slice-window`'s count equality becomes `≥`, with the excess recorded as `:yin.k/dead-prefix n` in the lift's diagnostics, never in the frame); `lower-stack` produces no prefix and recomputes bases cumulatively (`stack-k` `:635-651` already does). Unobservable: law 1 on continuing to the reference result; law 2/4 since the relift of the lowered kernel has no prefix and the canonical form never had one. **6e item 6** either exhibits a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call (then the rule is exercised) or proves by the static check `layout(p) = [] at every tail-call pc of the fragment` that none exists (then the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector). Both are acceptance, not open design.\n\n**Register side**: `allocation` gives distinct slots to virtuals both in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` (`derive.cljc:127-130`), freeing only when the interval end `< pc` (`:140-143`), so `virt(p, s)` over `L(p)`'s image is single-valued at every pc; `register-window` over `L(p)` for a run; `write-back`-style nil fill outside `L(p)`. Unchanged from v1; the review concurs; it is still a check (§3.9 6d-a item 7).\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Order per point** (reference lane, 6d-a; backends, 6e): at boundary `S_i` of the stepping source: (1) **lift**; (2) **lower** into a fresh receiver of an **isolated world** (§ below); (3) **immediate relift** of the receiver and **law 2** (`canonical-under` root equality with the lift; no execution in between); (4) **law 6** on every lifted frame (plus the two window tampers); (5) **run the receiver to the end** in its world, **law 1** (result and effect trace equal the reference's, after `normalize`'s fresh-identity correspondence); (6) negative controls kept: cursor positions and descriptors, windows and lexical values tampered are refused or differ as today.\n\n**Isolated worlds**: each point builds the receiver's composition and streams fresh from the case's **deterministic script** (the same scripted inputs the reference run saw, fed to the receiver's own streams); the stepping source's world is never shared with a receiver; fresh identities normalise through the existing correspondence (`normalize`, `:238`). The reference result and trace are computed once per case on a pristine world.\n\n**\"Every pc\"** means every boundary of the chosen executions (the stepping driver's `S_0 … S_end` for each case and each wrapper), not every CFG-reachable pc nor every possible activation. Coverage is pinned by `(segment-address, pc, structural-class)` where the class is one of: body entry, branch taken, branch untaken, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture (`:current-continuation`), resume, park, FFI park, scheduler product (active + waits, active + ready, blocked + ready), install child running; the harness asserts each class is reached at least once on the corpus and pins the point count per case per host. The static all-pc checks (§3.5) and the dynamic pass are complementary; neither proves a universal theorem and the docs say so (§3.7 \"claims that are checks\").\n\n**Refusal oracle (6e)**: for each case and point, the expected register-target refusal is computed by an **independent traversal in the test** over the decoded task: any `:prim` row reachable from the `run`'s window or lexical bindings, from any `K` frame's window or bindings, from any wait's or ready's state, or from any parked state. Assert per direction: exactly the expected points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before any attach (attach count zero), and **every other point transfers** and continues to the reference result. The other obstructions (image collision, installs, free-env procedure, retained FFI) are asserted on their existing fixtures and stay in the documented limit list.\n\n### 3.7 Amended document text (exact)\n\n10:FAIL in (sampled-returned-boundaries) (safepoint_harness_test.cljc:2346)\n13:6d-a quick boundary counts JVM: [{:row 1, :segment :bare, :measured 42, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive 22, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 39}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :non-tail-call}, :excluded-leading [], :park 0, :halt 1} {:row 2, :segment :bare, :measured 11, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive nil, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 8}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :non-tail-call}, :excluded-leading [], :park 0, :halt 1} {:row 3, :segment :bare, :measured 10, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive nil, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 7}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :non-tail-call :ffi-park}, :excluded-leading [], :park 2, :halt 1} {:row 4, :segment :bare, :measured 36, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive nil, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 33}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :non-tail-call :ffi-park}, :excluded-leading [], :park 28, :halt 1} {:row 5, :segment :bare, :measured 28, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive 13, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 25}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :non-tail-call}, :excluded-leading [], :park 0, :halt 1} {:row 6, :segment :bare, :measured 23, :samples {:body-entry 0, :after-primitive 14, :before-park 2, :branch nil, :last-return 20}, :coverage #{:return :body-entry :tail-tra